Write two lines of code: <p><code>1) Create an object dynamically: [ 1 ]</code> - Assume the class is called CBook and the object is called book. - The parameter list of the constructor is empty. - Please make a space before and after the equal sign. <code>2) Avoid memory leak: [ 2 ]</code></p> <pre><code>[1] - CBook* book = new CBook();
[2] - delete book;
</code></pre>

Write a line of code to create an int array dynamically. The size of the array is stored in the already existing variable called size. The name of the array is called array. Don't forget to free the memory! <pre><code>Create the array: [ 1 ]
Free the memory: [ 2 ]
</code></pre> <pre><code>[1] - int* array = new int[size];
[2] - delete array;
</code></pre>

Imagine you’re writing a getter function but the return type is void. How else could you return the value? <pre><code>[1] - 
Display the variable in the console?
Assign it to a private variable using a different setter?
</code></pre>  <p>Incorrect.</p>   <p>Correction</p> <pre><code>class Example
{
private:
int value = 30;
public:
void getValue(int&amp; result) // Pass by Reference so no variable copy.
{
result = value // change result to value using Reference (memory address)
}
};

int main()
{
Example obj;
int number;
obj.getValue(number); // changes the value using the private variable value in the Example class.
cout &lt;&lt; number; // 30
}
</code></pre>

What is the output of the following code? Watch out for the blanks! <pre><code>char stringArr[] = "I love C++";

char* ptr = stringArr;
++ptr;
cout &lt;&lt; ptr &lt;&lt; endl;
ptr += 2;
cout &lt;&lt; ptr &lt;&lt; endl;
cout &lt;&lt; --ptr &lt;&lt; endl;
</code></pre>  <p>Fail</p> <pre><code>[1] - 'I'
[2] - 'o'
[3] - 'space'
</code></pre>  <p>Null-Terminated (/0) Strings</p> <p>I was not previously aware that C++ also store the null character in strings at the end to mark the end.</p> <p><code>cout &lt;&lt; ptr</code> prints the c-string starting at the ptr address until it reaches /0.</p> <p>I was not aware/had forgotten about this behaviour in cout, and had mistaken it as a pointer behaviour, which it is not.</p>  <p>My mistake was assuming the pointer was a single index. I forgot that cout prints the rest of the null-terminated string starting from that character. To only print the single character the pointer would have to be dereferenced to the single character. (i.e. <code>*ptr</code>)</p>   <p>Success</p> <pre><code>[1] - ' love C++'
[2] - 'ove C++'
[3] - 'love C++'
</code></pre>

Which of the following lines of code are problematic? <p><code>Suppose ptr is of type int* and i is of type int.</code></p> <pre><code>A - ptr = &amp;i;

B - i = &amp;ptr;

C - *i = ptr;

D - ptr = ptr + &amp;i;

E - i = ptr;

F - ptr = ptr + ptr;

G - &amp;i = *ptr;

H - ptr = ptr + i;
</code></pre>

What is the output of the following program: <pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
using namespace std;

class Animal
﻿{
public:
    virtual void print()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "print Animal class" &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    void show()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "show Animal class" &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
};

class Hamster : public Animal
{
public:
    void print()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "print Hamster class" &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    void show()
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; "show Hamster class" &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Animal* pAnimal;
    Hamster hamster;
    pAnimal = &amp;hamster;

    pAnimal-&gt;print();

    pAnimal-&gt;show();
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>Output Line 1: [ 1 ]
Output Line 2: [ 2 ]
</code></pre>

